        379.7677001953125,
        616.7063598632812
      ],
      "direction": [
        1.0,
        0.0
      ]
    },
    {
      "text": "(5)",
      "bbox": [
        509.27996826171875,
        607.193603515625,
        522.0,
        616.9463500976562
      ],
      "direction": [
        1.0,
        0.0
      ]
    },
    {
      "text": "where e is the Euler number, then a minimizer of (4), if it exists, is a feasible solution to (3) with",
      "bbox": [
        89.99996948242188,
        632.9720458984375,
        522.0046997070312,
        642.6593627929688
      ],
      "direction": [
        1.0,
        0.0
      ]
    },
    {
      "text": "probability ≥1 −δ.",
      "bbox": [
        89.99996948242188,
        645.91015625,
        179.0182647705078,
        656.2083740234375
      ],
      "direction": [
        1.0,
        0.0
      ]
    },
    {
      "text": "While the convexity requirements on J and g can be restrictive, there is a hidden freedom: the",
      "bbox": [
        106.93598175048828,
        668.1920776367188,
        522.0004272460938,
        678.119384765625
      ],
      "direction": [
        1.0,
        0.0
      ]
    },
    {
      "text": "random variable Z in the probabilistic constraint may have arbitrary support. In our case, we will",
      "bbox": [
        89.99998474121094,
        681.7420654296875,
        522.0033569335938,
        691.6693725585938
      ],
      "direction": [
        1.0,
        0.0
      ]
    },
    {
      "text": "choose a random variable whose support is R[t0,t1].",
      "bbox": [
